"A log-log speedup for exponent one-fifth deterministic integer factorisation (https://arxiv.org/html/2105.11105v1)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2105.11105v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 484\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15: \nL16:   1. cite5†Abstract. L17:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L18:   3. cite7†2 Preliminaries L19:   4. cite8†3 A variant of Lehman’s result L20:   5. cite9†4 The main algorithm L21:   6. cite10†References L22: cite11†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L23: \nL24: arXiv:2105.11105v1 [math.NT] 24 May 2021\nL25: # A log-log speedup for exponent one-fifth deterministic integer factorisation Thanks: The first author was supported by the Australian Research Council (grant FT160100219).Thanks: SBA Research (SBA-K1) is a COMET Centre within the framework of COMET – Competence Centers for Excellent Technologies Programme and funded by BMK, BMDW, and the federal state of Vienna. The COMET Programme is managed by FFG\nL26: David Harvey Address: School of Mathematics and Statistics, University of New South Wales, Sydney NSW 2052, Australia Email address: d.harvey@unsw.edu.au and Markus Hittmeir Address: SBA Research, Floragasse 7, A-1040 Vienna Email address: mhittmeir@sba-research.org Dedicated to Richard Brent on the occasion of his $(3\\times 5^{2})$-th birthday\nL27: \nL28: © 2021, David Harvey and Markus Hittmeir\nL29: ###### Abstract.\nL30: \nL31: Building on techniques recently introduced by the second author, and further developed by the first author, we show that a positive integer $N$ may be rigorously and deterministically factored into primes in at most\nL32: \nL33:  | $$O\\left(\\frac{N^{1/5}\\log^{16/5}N}{(\\log\\log N)^{3/5}}\\right)$$  |\nL34: \nL35: bit operations. This improves on the previous best known result by a factor of $(\\log\\log N)^{3/5}$.\nL36: \nL37: ###### 2020 Mathematics Subject Classification\nL38: \nL39: Primary 11Y05\nL40: ## 1. Introduction\nL41: The aim of this paper is to further refine a method for integer factorisation that was recently introduced by the second author [cite12†Hit20 ], and subsequently improved and simplified by the first author [cite13†Har20 ]. We insist that all algorithms under discussion be deterministic and that all complexity bounds be proved rigorously. Faster factoring algorithms are known if one allows randomisation, heuristic arguments or quantum computers; see [cite14†CP05 , cite15†Len00 , cite16†Wag13 , cite17†Rie94 ].\nL42: We write $\\FF(N)$ for the time required to compute the prime factorisation of an integer $N\\geqslant 2$, where “time” means “bit operations”, i.e., the number of steps performed by a deterministic Turing machine with a fixed, finite number of linear tapes [cite18†Pap94 ]. All integers are assumed to be encoded in the usual binary representation.\nL43: Prior to [cite12†Hit20 ], the best known asymptotic bounds for $\\FF(N)$ were of the form $N^{1/4+o(1)}$. These were achieved by methods going back to Pollard, Strassen and Coppersmith; for further references see [cite13†Har20 ]. The algorithm presented in [cite12†Hit20 ] was the first to break the $1/4$ barrier, achieving $\\FF(N)=N^{2/9+o(1)}$. Shortly afterwards the first author made further progress on the exponent [cite13†Har20 ], showing that $\\FF(N)=O(N^{1/5}\\log^{16/5}N)$.\nL44: The main result of the present paper is the following incremental improvement.\nL45: ###### Theorem 1.1.\nL46: \nL47: There is a deterministic integer factorisation algorithm achieving\nL48: \nL49:  | $$\\FF(N)=O\\left(\\frac{N^{1/5}\\log^{16/5}N}{(\\log\\log N)^{3/5}}\\right).$$  |\nL50: Note that if $N$ has three or more prime factors (counting repetitions), then at least one factor is bounded above by $N^{1/3}$, and it is well known that such a factor may be found in time $N^{1/6+o(1)}$ (see for example [cite13†Har20 , Prop. 2.5]). Using this observation one easily reduces the proof of Theorem cite19†1.1 to the case that $N$ is either prime or semiprime, i.e., a product of two distinct primes. For details of the reduction, see the proof of [cite13†Har20 , Thm. 1.1].\nL51: For the rest of the paper, we assume that $N$ is either prime or semiprime, say $N=pq$ where $p<q$.\nL52: The strategy of [cite13†Har20 ] may be outlined as follows. Fix some integer $\\alpha$ coprime to $N$. Since $p\\equiv 1\\pmod{p-1}$, Fermat’s little theorem implies that $\\alpha^{aq+bp}\\equiv\\alpha^{aN+b}\\pmod{p}$ for any $a,b\\in\\mathbb{Z}$. If we can recover $aq+bp$, for known coefficients $a,b\\neq 0$, then it is easy to deduce $p$ and $q$, since we know the product $pq=N$ (see Lemma cite20†2.4 ).\nL53: Now, it was observed by Lehman [cite21†Leh74 ] (following ideas going back to Lawrence [cite22†Law95 ]) that if $a$ are $b$ are small integers, chosen so that $a/b$ is a good rational approximation to the unknown $p/q$, then $aq+bp$ will be especially close to $(4abN)^{1/2}$. This suggests rewriting the congruence as\nL54:  | $$\\alpha^{aq+bp-\\lfloor(4abN)^{1/2}\\rfloor}\\equiv\\alpha^{aN+b-\\lfloor(4abN)^{1/2}\\rfloor}\\pmod{p}.$$  |\nL55: When $a/b\\approx p/q$, the left hand side has the form $\\alpha^{i}$ where $i$ is “small”. Consequently, to solve for $aq+bp$, it suffices to find a collision modulo $p$ between the “giantsteps” $\\alpha^{aN+b-\\lfloor(4abN)^{1/2}\\rfloor}\\pmod{N}$, where $a/b$ ranges over some sufficiently dense set of rational numbers, and the “babysteps” $\\alpha^{i}\\pmod{N}$, where $i$ ranges over small integers.\nL56: This collision-finding problem may be attacked using tools from fast polynomial arithmetic, and [cite13†Har20 ] shows that this strategy (after filling in many details left out in this rough sketch) leads to the bound $\\FF(N)=O(N^{1/5}\\log^{16/5}N)$.\nL57: The new algorithm in this paper follows the same basic plan described above, but utilises the additional information that $p$ and $q$ cannot themselves be divisible by small primes. We modify the algorithm so that it restricts attention to candidates for $p$ that are coprime to $m\\coloneqq 2\\times 3\\times 5\\times\\cdots\\times p_{d}\\ll N^{1/2}$, i.e., $m$ is the product of the first $d$ primes for suitable $d$.\nL58: The number of possible values of $p$ modulo $m$ is $\\phi(m)$ (the Euler totient function), and for suitable choice of $m=N^{O(1)}$, Mertens’ theorem (see Lemma cite23†2.6 ) implies that the ratio $\\phi(m)/m$ decays like $1/\\log\\log N$. This is the source of the log-log savings over [cite13†Har20 ].\nL59: Actually, for technical reasons we reorganise the algorithm considerably: instead of defining the giantsteps by taking all pairs $(a,b)$ in some range, as is done in [cite13†Har20 ], we use algorithms for finding short lattice vectors to compute suitable values for $a$ and $b$ for each giantstep.\nL60: Of course it is well known that sieving on small primes in this way often leads to $(\\log\\log N)$-type speedups. For example, in the context of deterministic integer factorisation, this idea was used in [cite24†CH14 ] to improve the complexity of the factoring algorithm of [cite25†BGS07 ] by a factor of $(\\log\\log N)^{1/2}$.\nL61: ## 2. Preliminaries\nL62: \nL63: For $n$ a positive integer, we define $\\lg n\\coloneqq\\lfloor\\log n/\\log 2\\rfloor+1$. Observe that $\\lg n\\geqslant 1$ for all $n\\geqslant 1$, that $\\lg n=\\Theta(\\log n)$ for $n\\geqslant 2$, and that $\\lg n$ may be computed in time $O(\\lg n)$.\nL64: \nL65: We recall several facts about integer arithmetic. All results stated here without specific references may be found in textbooks such as [cite26†vzGG13 ] or [cite27†BZ11 ].\nL66: Let $n\\geqslant 1$, and assume that we are given integers $x$ and $y$ such that $|x|,|y|\\leqslant 2^{n}$. We may compute $x+y$ and $x-y$ in time $O(n)$. We write $\\MM(n)$ for the cost of computing the product $xy$; it was shown recently that $\\MM(n)=O(n\\lg n)$ [cite28†HvdH21 ]. If $y>0$, we may compute the quotients $\\lfloor x/y\\rfloor$ and $\\lceil x/y\\rceil$, and therefore also the residue of $x$ modulo $y$ in the interval $[0,y)$, in time $O(\\MM(n))$.\nL67: More generally, for a fixed positive rational number $u/v$, and assuming that $x,y>0$, we may compute $\\lfloor(x/y)^{u/v}\\rfloor$ and $\\lceil(x/y)^{u/v}\\rceil$ in time $O(\\MM(n))$. We may compute $g\\coloneqq\\gcd(x,y)$, and if desired find $u,v\\in\\mathbb{Z}$ such that $ux+vy=g$ (i.e., solve the extended GCD problem), in time $O(\\MM(n)\\lg n)=O(n\\lg^{2}n)$.\nL68: Next we consider modular arithmetic. Let $N\\geqslant 2$. We write $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ for the ring $\\mathbb{Z}/N\\mathbb{Z}$ of integers modulo $N$. Elements of $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ will always be represented by their residues in the interval $[0,N)$, so occupy $O(\\lg N)$ bits of storage. We write $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}$ for the group of units of $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$, i.e., the subset of those $x\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ for which $\\gcd(x,N)=1$.\nL69: Given $x,y\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$, we may compute $x\\pm y\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ in time $O(\\lg N)$, and $xy\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ in time $O(\\MM(\\lg N))=O(\\lg N\\lg\\lg N)$. Given $x\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ and an integer $m\\geqslant 1$, we may compute $x^{m}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ in time $O(\\MM(\\lg N)\\lg m)$, using the “repeated squaring” algorithm.\nL70: We may test whether $x\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}$, and if so compute $x^{-1}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}$, in time $O(\\MM(\\lg N)\\lg\\lg N)=O(\\lg N(\\lg\\lg N)^{2})$, by solving the extended GCD problem for $x$ and $N$. If the gcd is not $1$ or $N$, and if $N=pq$ is semiprime, then we immediately recover $p$ and $q$ in time $O(\\MM(\\lg N))$.\nL71: The next few results are taken from the previous papers [cite29†Hit18 ] and [cite13†Har20 ].\nL72: ###### Lemma 2.1.\nL73: \nL74: Let $n\\geqslant 1$ with $n=O(N)$. Given as input $v_{1},\\ldots,v_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$, we may compute the polynomial $f(x)=(x-v_{1})\\cdots(x-v_{n})\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}[x]$ in time $O(n\\lg^{3}N)$.\nL75: \nL76: ###### Proof.\nL77: \nL78: This is exactly [cite13†Har20 , Lem. 2.3]; the proof depends on a standard application of a product tree. By “compute a polynomial” we mean compute a list of its coefficients. ∎\nL79: ###### Remark 2.2.\nL80: \nL81: In the above lemma, the hypothesis “$n=O(N)$” means that for every constant $C>0$, the lemma holds for all $n$ and $N$ in the region $n<CN$, where the implied big-$O$ constant in the target bound $O(n\\lg^{3}N)$ may depend on $C$. For the rest of the paper, a similar remark applies whenever we use the big-$O$ notation in this way.\nL82: ###### Lemma 2.3.\nL83: \nL84: Given as input an element $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}$, positive integers $n,\\kappa=O(N)$, and $f\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}[x]$ of degree $n$, we may compute $f(1),f(\\alpha),\\ldots,f(\\alpha^{\\kappa-1})\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ in time $O((n+\\kappa)\\lg^{2}N)$.\nL85: \nL86: ###### Proof.\nL87: \nL88: This is exactly [cite13†Har20 , Lem. 2.4]. The proof relies on Bluestein’s algorithm [cite30†Blu70 ], which saves a logarithmic factor over a general multipoint evaluation. ∎\nL89: ###### Lemma 2.4.\nL90: \nL91: Given as input an integer $N\\geqslant 2$ such that $N$ is either a prime or semiprime $pq$, and integers $a,b,u$ with at most $O(\\lg N)$ bits, we may test if $u$ is of the form $aq+bp$, and if so recover $p$ and $q$, in time $O(\\lg N\\lg\\lg N)$.\nL92: \nL93: ###### Proof.\nL94: \n----------------------------------------------------